From: Long Way Home <noreply@long-way-home.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 23/24] the engine's quit path asks the game module

The wall-clock exit (docs/omissions.md O14, docs/ship-model.md "The three clocks, and the two
exits"). The model has had `Suspend`, `leftStanding` and the catch-up since save version 43; what
was missing is the exit a player actually takes. The engine's quit path (Com_Quit_f) now asks the
game module what this quit means *before* it tears anything down, because the module is the only
thing that knows whether a ship is running and which of the two exits the player chose.

The logic is not in this tree. The bridge only hands the module the command (`ship quit`); the
decision -- leave the ship standing, or shut the simulation down -- is `module/ship/g_ship.cpp`,
gated by `g_shipQuit`, which is 0 by default and only read while `g_ship` is on. Inert, the hook
answers nothing and a quit is what it always was: the retail campaign, the expansion and Holomatch
are unaffected, and the shutdown reasons are taken before the hook re-tokenises the command line,
so even the strings in the client shutdown banner are unchanged.

It is an engine patch rather than a module one because `quit` is an engine console command: the
engine's Cmd_ExecuteString finds it in the command table and runs it directly, so the module never
sees it and cannot answer for itself.
---
diff --git a/EFAndroid-SP/app/jni/efcode/qcommon/common.c b/EFAndroid-SP/app/jni/efcode/qcommon/common.c
--- a/EFAndroid-SP/app/jni/efcode/qcommon/common.c
+++ b/EFAndroid-SP/app/jni/efcode/qcommon/common.c
@@ -401,13 +401,21 @@
 	// don't try to shutdown if we are in a recursive error
 	char *p = Cmd_Args( );
 	if ( !com_errorEntered ) {
+		// Long Way Home: the wall-clock exit (patches/0023). The game module is asked what this quit
+		// means before anything is torn down -- its logic lives in module/ and it answers nothing while
+		// the simulation is off, so a retail quit is unchanged. Asking it re-tokenises the command
+		// line, so the shutdown reasons are taken here, where they were read before.
+		char serverReason[256], clientReason[256];
+		Q_strncpyz( serverReason, p[0] ? p : "Server quit", sizeof( serverReason ) );
+		Q_strncpyz( clientReason, p[0] ? p : "Client quit", sizeof( clientReason ) );
+		{ extern void SP_EngineQuit( void ); SP_EngineQuit(); }
 		// Some VMs might execute "quit" command directly,
 		// which would trigger an unload of active VM error.
 		// Sys_Quit will kill this process anyways, so
 		// a corrupt call stack makes no difference
 		VM_Forced_Unload_Start();
-		SV_Shutdown(p[0] ? p : "Server quit");
-		CL_Shutdown(p[0] ? p : "Client quit", qtrue, qtrue);
+		SV_Shutdown( serverReason );
+		CL_Shutdown( clientReason, qtrue, qtrue );
 		VM_Forced_Unload_Done();
 		Com_Shutdown ();
 		FS_Shutdown(qtrue);
diff --git a/EFAndroid-SP/app/jni/sp/sp_bridge.cpp b/EFAndroid-SP/app/jni/sp/sp_bridge.cpp
--- a/EFAndroid-SP/app/jni/sp/sp_bridge.cpp
+++ b/EFAndroid-SP/app/jni/sp/sp_bridge.cpp
@@ -1778,6 +1778,16 @@
 extern "C" int SP_GameConsoleCommand(void){
     return ( ge && g_spActive && ge->ConsoleCommand && ge->ConsoleCommand() ) ? 1 : 0;
 }
+// Long Way Home: the wall-clock exit (patches/0023). The engine's quit path (common.c, Com_Quit_f)
+// calls this before it tears anything down, so the game module gets the last word on what the quit
+// means: leave the ship standing (she keeps her own time) or shut the simulation down. The bridge
+// only hands the module the command -- the decision is the module's (module/ship/g_ship.cpp), and a
+// module that is not running answers nothing, so a retail quit is byte-for-byte what it always was.
+extern "C" void SP_EngineQuit(void){
+    if(!(ge && g_spActive)) return;
+    Cmd_TokenizeString("ship quit");
+    SP_GameConsoleCommand();
+}
 // True while a save-load/transition is queued (g_transMap set) but the SP cgame hasn't come up yet.
 // The load watchdog (sp_integration.c) uses this to retry a starved/dropped efsptrans before it
 // gives up and bounces to the main menu.
